          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Sump Pump Installation Cost",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/sump-pump-installation-cost/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does it take to install a sump pump?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Replacing a pump in an existing pit usually takes one to three hours. Installing a new pit, including cutting the slab, digging, setting the liner, running a discharge line and patching concrete, typically takes a full day or more. Adding interior drain tile can stretch the job to several days depending on basement size."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does homeowners insurance cover sump pump replacement?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Standard policies usually do not pay to replace a worn-out pump, and many exclude water damage from sump backups unless you add a specific endorsement. That endorsement is often inexpensive. Check your policy wording and ask your agent about sump pump or water backup coverage before you need it. Keep receipts for the new pump."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do you need a plumber to install a sump pump?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not always. A handy homeowner can replace a pump in an existing pit. New pits, new circuits and buried discharge lines are better left to professionals, and some areas require licensed work or permits for them. Check local rules, especially for electrical work and connections to municipal drainage systems. A licensed pro also warranties the work."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How often should a sump pump be replaced?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Many pumps last about seven to ten years, though heavy use can shorten that and light use can extend it. Test the pump a few times a year by filling the pit with water, and consider replacing it proactively as it nears the end of that range, especially if your basement is finished."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Where should a sump pit be located?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually at the lowest point of the basement and near an exterior wall so the discharge line has a short run outside. It should be away from foundation footings and accessible for maintenance. If you are adding interior drain tile, the pit goes where the drainage system can slope to it."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a cast iron sump pump worth the extra money?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most homes with regular water, yes. Cast iron pumps shed heat into the surrounding water better than plastic, which helps the motor under heavy cycling, and they generally hold up longer. For an occasional-use pit in an unfinished basement, a quality thermoplastic pump can be a reasonable budget choice."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a sump pump drain into the sewer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Often not. Many municipalities prohibit connecting sump pumps to sanitary sewer lines because the extra water can overwhelm treatment plants and cause sewage backups. Some allow connection to storm sewers. Ask your local building or public works department before routing the discharge anywhere other than your yard. Fines can apply for illegal connections."
          }
        }
